// Authored decision guides. Prices, availability and stylist credentials are not inferred.
export const pages = [];
const add = (slug,title,group,service,intro,sections,questions) => pages.push({slug,title,group,service,intro,sections,questions});

add('bridal-party-hair-pittsburgh','Bridal Party Hair in Pittsburgh: Organize the Group','Bridal and events','bridal-hair-pittsburgh',
'A bridal-party hair booking needs a group plan as well as individual style choices. Craft Collective offers bridal-party styling. Contact the salon with the wedding date, location, number of people and required finish time before assuming the party can fit into a normal individual appointment.',[
['Build a complete service list','List each person who wants styling and indicate whether they are considering an updo, a half-up finish, a blowout or another event style. Include the bride, bridesmaids, mothers and other guests who need appointments. Different starting hair and styles can require different time blocks. If some people are undecided, say so early. The salon needs an accurate group count to discuss staffing and timing rather than a final headcount that arrives shortly before the wedding.'],
['Work backward from the actual departure time','Identify when the party must be ready for photographs or leave for the venue. Build in dressing, travel and any other services scheduled that morning. Ask the salon how the hair sequence fits those commitments and who should be ready first. A wedding timeline should account for more than the ceremony time. Do not promise everyone the same appointment length or a simultaneous start before the team has reviewed the group’s needs and current availability.'],
['Share references without requiring identical hair','A coordinated bridal party does not require every person to wear an identical style on different hair lengths and textures. Explain the shared direction, such as polished updos or soft waves, then let the individual plan reflect the person’s starting hair. Ask about preparation, accessories and added hair before the day. Provide clear instructions to the group so nobody arrives expecting washing, extension placement or another step that was not included in the proposed schedule.'],
['Confirm the group agreement','Ask for the price and inclusions for each service, the confirmed location, any travel arrangements and the current booking and cancellation policies. Identify one person responsible for communicating changes. Confirm party-size requirements, pricing, travel charges and capacity with the team. A written confirmation from the salon should settle the service list and timing before the wedding schedule is circulated to everyone involved.']
],[['Can mothers and wedding guests join the booking?','Craft Collective lists styling for wedding-party members. Confirm the full group and required services when requesting availability.'],['Is there a fixed bridal-party package price?','Request the current proposal for your party. Ask about the applicable group arrangements and service inclusions.']]);

add('on-location-wedding-hair-pittsburgh','On-Location Wedding Hair in Pittsburgh: What to Confirm','Bridal and events','bridal-hair-pittsburgh',
'Choosing wedding hair at a salon or at your getting-ready location changes the logistics of the morning. Craft Collective’s bridal service page discusses salon and on-location styling. Ask the team to confirm travel availability and the individual arrangement for your date before building the wedding timeline around it.',[
['Provide the full location information','Share the venue or getting-ready address, the wedding date and the time the party needs to be finished. Explain whether the space is a home, hotel or event venue and whether access is limited to particular hours. Ask who needs to approve the use of the room. The salon can evaluate the request more accurately with the actual address and schedule than with a general description such as Pittsburgh wedding or nearby venue.'],
['Ask about the workspace','Discuss room access, lighting, suitable seating and the electrical setup needed for the proposed styling. Ask what the stylist brings and what the venue should provide. If makeup, photography and dressing will happen in the same room, consider how the space will be shared. Confirm the setup directly rather than assuming a small hotel room can accommodate the full party and several vendors at once. A practical workspace supports a calmer schedule.'],
['Compare travel with salon appointments','A salon visit may provide an established styling environment, while on-location work may reduce travel for the wedding party. The choice depends on timing, group size and confirmed arrangements. Ask about all applicable charges and the number of stylists proposed for the group. Ask which destinations the team can accommodate and what travel charges apply. Those details must be agreed with Craft Collective for the specific date and destination.'],
['Connect the arrangement to the trial','Use the bridal trial to finalize the style and discuss accessories, preparation and how the day will run at the chosen location. Confirm whether the same stylist is scheduled and who handles any changes to the timeline. Ask for current cancellation and booking policies. Keep the confirmed address, contact person and finish time together in the wedding schedule. An on-location inquiry is not a reservation until the salon has confirmed the arrangement and service plan.']
],[['Can Craft Collective travel to any Pittsburgh venue?','Contact the salon with the address and date. The team can confirm whether it can accommodate the destination and schedule.'],['Are travel charges included in bridal styling?','Ask for the complete proposal. Styling, travel and group arrangements should be clarified separately when applicable.']]);

add('blonde-colorist-pittsburgh','Choosing a Blonde Colorist in Pittsburgh: Plan the Result','Specialist decisions','balayage-pittsburgh',
'A blonde consultation should describe the brightness, tone and placement you want on your existing hair. Craft Collective’s published menu includes blonding, platinum, lived-in blonde and highlights. Call for help selecting a colorist and appointment when the intended change is larger than routine maintenance.',[
['Separate brightness from tone','A cooler shade is not necessarily a lighter shade. Identify whether you want more visible lightness, less warmth or a different contrast with your root color. Bring a current photograph and a reference taken in reasonably comparable lighting. Filters and editing can make a blonde appear different from its real-life finish. Ask the colorist to explain the target in terms of the work required on your hair instead of relying only on names such as icy or creamy.'],
['Discuss the hair’s previous processing','Tell the stylist about old highlights, permanent color and at-home products. The ends may not have the same starting condition as new growth. Ask how the plan accounts for those differences and whether the first goal should be a balanced result before adding more brightness. A photograph of another person’s transformation does not establish how many sessions yours will take. A realistic blonde plan should include what the stylist recommends avoiding or postponing.'],
['Choose the placement for your everyday style','Show the way you part your hair and how it looks tied back. Ask about brightness around the face, depth at the root and contrast underneath the hair. A full bright blonde and a rooted lived-in blonde create different maintenance decisions. If you enjoy dimensional color, explain which darker areas you want to retain. The result should still make sense when you wear your ordinary hairstyle rather than only the polished finish in the reference.'],
['Use the consultation to select the booking','Review the colorist’s relevant work through the team and gallery, then ask which appointment fits the proposed plan. Confirm toning, finishing and any haircut in the estimate. Ask which return visits maintain the chosen tone and placement. Do not book an undersized highlight slot for a full blonde transition without discussing it. For Craft Collective, specify North Hills or Canonsburg and call when the menu label does not explain the work you want.']
],[['Can a brunette become platinum in one visit?','The stylist needs to assess current color, history and condition before setting the first achievable goal.'],['Which blonde stylist should I choose?','Review the team’s work and call with your current hair and desired result for help selecting an appropriate appointment.']]);

add('fine-hair-haircuts-pittsburgh','Haircuts for Fine Hair in Pittsburgh: Shape Without Guesswork','Specialist decisions','haircuts-pittsburgh',
'Fine hair describes the individual strands, while density describes how much hair is present. Those are different starting points for a haircut conversation. At Craft Collective, explain whether your concern is a flat crown, thin-looking ends, lack of movement or a shape that loses its outline quickly.',[
['Identify where the shape needs support','Show the stylist the part of your hair that looks less full than you want. A perimeter that feels sparse may call for a different decision from a crown that falls flat. Ask how the proposed length and layers affect each area. More layering is not a universal answer to fine hair. A cut should balance the outline you like with the movement you want, considering the density actually present in each section.'],
['Compare bluntness and layering','A stronger perimeter can create a more defined visual outline, while selected layers can change movement and the way the hair falls. The useful balance depends on your starting length and styling habits. Ask the stylist to explain where weight will be retained and where it will be removed. A reference with thick hair may need adaptation rather than exact copying. If you want a fringe, discuss the amount of hair it would use and its effect on the surrounding shape.'],
['Test the daily routine','Describe whether you air-dry, blow-dry or use hot tools and how much time you want to spend on the finish. Ask how the new shape will look with that routine. Product choice can contribute to the finish, but it does not replace a suitable outline. Request a demonstration of the most useful styling step rather than an extensive routine you are unlikely to repeat. Also explain whether the hair needs to tie back comfortably.'],
['Keep cosmetic planning separate from new shedding','A haircut can change the appearance of fullness; it does not diagnose or treat a medical cause of hair loss. If you notice new shedding, scalp discomfort or a significant unexplained change, discuss it with a healthcare professional. For the salon visit, focus on the shape and finish that work with the hair you currently have. Ask what to book when the perimeter needs refreshing and whether a smaller maintenance trim preserves the intended outline.']
],[['Are fine hair and thin hair the same thing?','They are often used loosely, but strand thickness and overall density are different details to explain during the consultation.'],['Should fine hair always avoid layers?','No universal rule fits every haircut. Ask where the proposed layering adds movement and where the stylist will retain a stronger outline.']]);

add('short-haircuts-pittsburgh','Short Haircuts in Pittsburgh: Bobs, Pixies and Maintenance','Specialist decisions','haircuts-pittsburgh',
'A short haircut changes the outline around the face, ears and neckline. Craft Collective offers precision haircut consultations. Bring references showing more than the front view and discuss how short you are comfortable going in each area before committing to a bob, pixie or cropped shape.',[
['Use references to explain the outline','A bob, a pixie and a longer crop can look similar from one angle but create very different shapes at the back. Show the side and neckline when possible. Identify whether you want softness around the ears, a defined fringe or enough length to tuck sections back. Explain the features you want to emphasize without treating a face-shape label as a rule. The stylist can adapt the reference to the way your own hair grows and falls.'],
['Discuss growth patterns and texture','Tell the stylist which areas resist your preferred direction or need extra styling. A cowlick, natural wave or dense section can influence how a shorter outline behaves. Ask what the shape will look like without the finished salon styling and whether the intended fringe needs daily attention. Shorter hair is not automatically lower maintenance. The tradeoff is often less length to handle but more attention to the details of the outline.'],
['Plan the first change thoughtfully','If you are moving from long hair, explain which lengths feel essential and which you are ready to remove. A staged approach can help you evaluate a bob before deciding on a much shorter crop, but it is not necessary for everyone. Ask how the proposed shape would grow if you later change your mind. The consultation should make the first result clear rather than assume that a dramatic reference means every section should be cut equally short.'],
['Book maintenance for the intended shape','Ask which areas will need refreshing first and what service category to select for the next visit. A fringe refresh or neckline adjustment is different from repeating a complete reshape; confirm what the salon offers. Review styling steps, products and tools only as they apply to your chosen finish. At Craft Collective, call if you need help finding a stylist for a major short-hair change and confirm the estimate for the appointment you select.']
],[['Is a pixie always easier to style than long hair?','It depends on texture, growth patterns and the finish you want. Discuss the everyday routine before cutting.'],['Can I book a major short haircut as a trim?','Explain the intended change first so the salon can select an appropriate appointment and consultation time.']]);

add('evening-hair-appointments-north-hills','Evening Hair Appointments in North Hills Pittsburgh','Appointment planning','hair-color-pittsburgh',
'Craft Collective’s published North Hills hours extend to 9 p.m. Tuesday through Thursday. Evening opening hours can be useful for guests planning around work, but the latest available start depends on the service and stylist. Check the North Hills booking calendar or call before arranging your visit.',[
['Allow time for the full service','A salon that closes at 9 p.m. cannot necessarily begin a long color appointment shortly before closing. The available start times should account for consultation, preparation, the main service and finishing. Tell the salon whether you want routine maintenance or a significant change, and whether a haircut is part of the plan. Choosing a shorter online service solely because it has a later opening may leave insufficient time for the work you actually want.'],
['Plan around the correct studio','North Hills online booking is associated with the studio at 2014D Babcock Blvd, Pittsburgh. Canonsburg appointments at 115 W Pike St are arranged by phone and should not be assumed to follow the same evening schedule. Check the location in your confirmation before traveling. If you are coming from Wexford, McCandless, Ross Township or Cranberry Township, plan the route for the actual appointment time rather than treating an estimated drive as guaranteed.'],
['Communicate a fixed deadline early','If you must leave by a particular time, mention it before reserving the service. Ask whether the requested goal fits the available appointment or whether a smaller first step is more practical. A late departure concern should not appear for the first time while color is processing. For an event, allow additional time for travel and dressing. The salon can help you understand the proposed appointment, but a published duration is not a guaranteed completion time.'],
['Confirm current calendar availability','Check the selected stylist’s openings and review the booking details before confirming. Evening demand and the roster can vary. This guide does not promise same-day availability, a particular stylist after work or an appointment at the latest published hour. If you cannot find the correct service or time online, call 724-514-7231. The team can advise on the current choices without requiring you to guess which menu category will accommodate your goal.']
],[['Is Craft Collective open until 9 every night?','The published North Hills hours list Tuesday through Thursday until 9 p.m. Confirm the current calendar for your service and date.'],['Does Canonsburg have the same late hours?','Canonsburg is scheduled by phone. Call to confirm that studio’s availability rather than using North Hills hours.']]);

add('last-minute-hair-appointments-pittsburgh','Last-Minute Hair Appointments in Pittsburgh: Check the Right Slot','Appointment planning','blowout-pittsburgh',
'If you need a Pittsburgh hair appointment soon, begin with the exact service, location and deadline. Craft Collective’s North Hills calendar shows online booking options; call for help selecting an appointment or arranging Canonsburg. This guide does not promise walk-ins, same-day openings or a cancellation list.',[
['Describe the smallest complete goal','Explain whether you need a haircut, a polished event finish, a root refresh or a larger color change. A last-minute request becomes easier to assess when the goal is specific. If you are flexible about the result, say which part is essential and which could wait. A blowout for an event and a corrective color appointment are very different scheduling requests. Do not choose a short category and plan to ask for substantially more work after arrival.'],
['Check the calendar without changing the service','Look for the correct service and stylist at the North Hills location. If an opening appears under a different service, call before assuming the same time can be used for your request. The calendar is organized around appointment requirements, not simply vacant minutes. Confirm the location and the service name in your reservation. For Canonsburg, call the salon so the team can advise on the actual availability and how the appointment should be arranged.'],
['Explain the event deadline','Share when you need to be finished and any travel afterward. Ask whether the available appointment can realistically accommodate the requested styling. If it cannot, discuss a different finish or a later appointment for the larger change. Avoid scheduling an unfamiliar major color transformation immediately before an important event without a consultation. The aim is to select a service you can understand and approve within the time available, rather than rely on a rushed assumption.'],
['Confirm policies and preparation','Ask what condition your hair should be in on arrival and whether accessories or added hair require advance discussion. Review the current cancellation and booking policies even when the appointment is soon. If your plans change, contact the salon through the stated process. Availability changes throughout the day, so a static article cannot serve as a live inventory. Use the current calendar or call 724-514-7231 for the appointment choices that are actually available.']
],[['Does Craft Collective guarantee same-day appointments?','No such guarantee is established here. Check current North Hills availability or call the salon.'],['Can I walk in for a color correction?','Craft Collective requires an in-person correction consultation. Call to discuss the appropriate first appointment and availability.']]);

add('hair-extensions-cost-pittsburgh','Hair Extensions Cost in Pittsburgh: Hair, Installation and Upkeep','Pricing and value','hair-extensions-pittsburgh',
'The complete extensions budget includes more than the installation appointment. Craft Collective requires an extension consultation to assess your natural hair, discuss the goal and plan the method and amount of hair. Ask for the initial quote and the expected maintenance costs as separate parts of the decision.',[
['Identify what the added hair must achieve','Fullness, added length and both together can require different amounts of hair. Describe the finish you want in a ponytail as well as with the hair down. Your existing density and the length difference matter to the plan. Ask how the proposed amount contributes to the blend and whether a smaller change could meet your goal. An installation advertised for one row or one quantity is not automatically equivalent to another person’s complete transformation.'],
['Separate the hair purchase from salon work','Ask whether the quote includes the added hair, installation, color matching, blending cut and styling. Confirm the length and amount quoted and whether any additional color work on your natural hair is recommended. Ask about payment and ordering policies directly before committing. Confirm the selected hair, payment terms and current ordering policies directly. The consultation should make each component clear enough that you understand what you are agreeing to buy.'],
['Budget for maintenance and removal','A useful plan explains how the selected method is maintained as your natural hair grows. Ask which follow-up appointments are required, what they cost and when the hair itself may need replacement. Removal is a separate question worth asking at the beginning. The longest possible wear time is not a useful goal if it conflicts with the stylist’s assessment of your hair. Follow the method-specific recommendation rather than extending the schedule to reduce the apparent monthly cost.'],
['Compare methods on an equivalent goal','Use the same target length and fullness when comparing tape-in, hand-tied and keratin-bond options. Ask how the initial price and maintenance differ for your actual plan. A lower installation fee can describe a different quantity or exclude hair that another quote includes. Confirm the particular stylist, studio and method availability with Craft Collective before ordering. The reliable cost comparison is the complete initial service plus the upkeep needed for the result you want.']
],[['Can I get an extension price without a consultation?','An accurate quote needs the method, amount and length of hair, color plan and installation requirements. Craft Collective requires a consultation.'],['Does installation include the hair itself?','Ask explicitly. Hair purchases, installation and maintenance should be itemized in the proposal rather than assumed to be included.']]);

add('keratin-treatment-cost-pittsburgh','Keratin Treatment Cost in Pittsburgh: Compare the Service','Pricing and value','keratin-treatment-pittsburgh',
'Keratin treatment prices should be compared alongside the actual smoothing service and aftercare. Craft Collective offers keratin smoothing consultations. Explain whether your goal is easier blow-drying, less visible frizz or a smoother finish, and ask what the proposed treatment is expected to do on your hair.',[
['Clarify the desired finish','Smoothing and a permanently straight appearance are different requests. Explain how much natural movement you want to retain and whether you normally wear the hair curly or smooth. A reference image cannot establish how a treatment will behave on your texture. Ask the stylist what change is realistic and which features of your current hair will remain. If you want only a polished finish for one event, compare a blowout before committing to a longer-lasting service.'],
['Ask which treatment is being quoted','Different salons may use different systems and include different preparation and finishing steps. Ask for the product or system name, the intended application and the aftercare instructions before comparing quotes. Share previous color, lightening and other chemical-service history. If you have sensitivities or concerns about ingredients, discuss the exact product with the salon before booking. This page does not promise that a treatment is suitable for every person or that all keratin systems are equivalent.'],
['Review the complete appointment','Confirm whether washing, preparation, application and finishing are included, and whether length or density changes the estimate. Ask how long to allow for the visit and which steps should be booked separately if you also want a haircut or color. The salon can advise on sequencing; do not assume multiple chemical services can be combined in the same appointment simply because the online schedule has an opening. The estimate should describe the approved service plan.'],
['Include aftercare in the budget','Ask which home products or handling steps are recommended for the treatment actually used, and whether they are optional purchases or part of the proposed package. Discuss when to reassess the result rather than relying on a guaranteed duration. Washing habits, texture and the chosen treatment can affect the experience. Craft Collective’s current quote should be confirmed directly with the salon; this guide does not invent a treatment price, brand-specific guarantee or mandatory product bundle.']
],[['Does keratin pricing include aftercare products?','Confirm the estimate. Product purchases and the salon treatment may be listed separately.'],['Is a smoothing treatment the same as a blowout?','No. A blowout is a styling service; discuss the intended treatment and longer-term implications separately.']]);

add('color-correction-cost-pittsburgh','Color Correction Cost in Pittsburgh: Understand the Estimate','Pricing and value','hair-color-pittsburgh',
'Corrective color pricing follows the work required to address your specific starting point. Craft Collective requires an in-person corrective-color consultation. The estimate should explain the first agreed result, the components of that appointment and whether a later visit may be needed.',[
['Describe the problem the quote addresses','An adjustment to unwanted warmth is different from removing repeated dark color or balancing uneven lightened sections. Tell the colorist what bothers you most and whether you want to preserve the existing brightness or change it. A useful estimate is connected to that particular correction. Ask the stylist to explain the first goal in everyday language, then make sure the quote describes the same work instead of a broad promise to fix everything.'],
['Ask how the price is calculated','The salon may organize correction estimates differently from ordinary color appointments. Ask whether the proposal is based on planned service components, time or another stated approach. Confirm the estimate before work begins and ask how any needed change will be discussed. Confirm the current billing approach directly with Craft Collective. What matters is having a clear agreement about the work and its cost.'],
['Distinguish one visit from a transition','If the long-term goal is significantly lighter than the current color, ask what can be achieved in the first appointment and what later sessions would aim to change. A first-visit price is not necessarily the cost of the entire transition. Request a description of likely next steps while recognizing that reassessment may change the plan. If the projected commitment exceeds your budget, discuss a smaller adjustment you would still be happy to wear.'],
['Check finishing and maintenance costs','Ask whether toning, cutting, a blow-dry or other relevant steps are included in the proposed total. Then ask what maintenance would follow once the correction goal is reached. Future routine care and future corrective work are different items. For the consultation, bring the color history you know and current photographs. Avoid applying another home color before the appointment without discussing it with the salon, since that can change both the starting point and the proposed work.']
],[['Why can’t a correction be quoted from the service name alone?','The starting color, condition and desired change determine the work. Craft Collective calls for an in-person assessment.'],['Will the first quote cover every future visit?','Ask. A staged change may involve separately planned appointments; the initial estimate should state the scope it covers.']]);

add('affordable-hair-salon-pittsburgh','Affordable Hair Salon Options in Pittsburgh: Plan Your Budget','Pricing and value','hair-color-pittsburgh',
'Looking for an affordable Pittsburgh salon starts with defining the result and the budget you can maintain. This guide helps you compare equivalent appointments and discuss smaller service plans with Craft Collective. It does not claim that Craft Collective has the lowest prices in the city.',[
['State a budget before choosing the plan','Explain what you would like to spend on the first visit and on later upkeep. Give the stylist your ideal result and identify the part that matters most. If a full transformation exceeds the budget, ask what smaller change could address that priority. A face-framing adjustment, a maintenance cut and an all-over change are different decisions. The salon should explain the result each option would provide rather than simply reducing time or scope without discussion.'],
['Compare the same finished appointment','Look at the full estimate, including the cut, tone and finishing steps that apply to your plan. A starting price for one component may not describe the complete appointment you expect. Ask whether the listed service is intended for a first visit or for maintenance on an existing result. Confirm any length, density or stylist differences in the quote. A useful comparison begins with equivalent goals and clearly stated inclusions at each salon.'],
['Account for the cost of upkeep','Consider how often the proposed shape or color needs attention and which appointments maintain it. A result with softer contrast may fit someone who cannot return as often, while a precise short shape may need more regular refreshes. Ask what will change visibly between visits and whether you would be comfortable with that change. The lowest first-visit total is not the only measure of affordability if the upkeep schedule does not fit your year-round budget.'],
['Choose from confirmed current options','Call Craft Collective to discuss the selected stylist, service and studio. Ask about current appointment options and pricing without assuming a promotion, student rate or new-talent discount exists. If you already have a service booked, discuss a budget change before arriving so the team can advise on a realistic alternative. A clear budget conversation makes it easier to agree on a plan you can enjoy and maintain without adding unplanned work during the visit.']
],[['Is Craft Collective the cheapest Pittsburgh salon?','This guide makes no citywide price ranking. Compare the current individual quote and the service included.'],['Can I ask for a lower-maintenance color plan?','Yes. Explain your preferred budget and visit frequency so the colorist can discuss a realistic option.']]);

add('hand-tied-extensions-pittsburgh','Hand-Tied Extensions in Pittsburgh: Consultation and Care','Extension methods','hair-extensions-pittsburgh',
'Craft Collective lists hand-tied extensions among its available methods. A consultation should establish whether a weft-based plan suits your natural hair, preferred hairstyles and desired fullness or length. Confirm the particular technique and stylist before ordering hair or reserving an installation.',[
['Discuss the foundation and coverage','Ask how the proposed wefts will be placed and where they will contribute fullness. The amount of hair and placement should fit your density and the result you want. Show the stylist how you wear your hair up and where you part it. A plan that blends well worn down may need additional discussion if a high ponytail is part of your everyday routine. Do not infer that a particular proprietary hand-tied method is offered from the general service name.'],
['Consider the blend as part of installation','Matching added hair involves more than choosing a color on a screen. Ask how the lengths, texture and dimension will fit your natural hair and whether a blending cut is planned. If you also want a substantial color change, discuss its sequence before the installation. The quote should clarify any separate work on your own hair. Bring current photographs that show both the overall shape and the ends so the consultation is based on the existing outline.'],
['Plan the maintenance from the beginning','Ask what happens at the recommended follow-up: which parts are adjusted, how the natural growth is assessed and whether the same hair can be reused under the specific plan. Maintenance intervals should come from the selected stylist rather than a universal online rule. Ask for the follow-up price and the removal process before installation. If you cannot attend the required appointments, discuss a different method or a smaller goal instead of planning to extend wear beyond the recommendation.'],
['Learn handling around the attachment','Request a demonstration of brushing, washing and drying appropriate to the method used. Ask how to support the attachments while detangling and what to do if a section feels uncomfortable or appears loose. Do not pull at or try to repair an attachment yourself. Contact the salon for guidance. A good consultation also covers your exercise, swimming and sleeping habits so the care plan matches real life instead of assuming every extension wearer follows the same routine.']
],[['Does hand-tied mean a specific certified brand method?','No. Confirm the exact method and any credentials directly with the stylist.'],['Can hand-tied extensions be used only for fullness?','Discuss that goal in the consultation. Amount, placement and suitability depend on the assessment of your natural hair.']]);

add('tape-in-extensions-pittsburgh','Tape-In Extensions in Pittsburgh: Fit, Blend and Maintenance','Extension methods','hair-extensions-pittsburgh',
'Tape-in extensions use flat attachment sections and can be discussed for length or fullness. Craft Collective lists tape-in extensions on its menu and requires a consultation. The decision should account for your natural density, preferred parting, styling habits and willingness to attend maintenance appointments.',[
['Evaluate placement on your own hair','Show the stylist where your hair is finest and which parts you wear exposed when it is tied back. Ask how the proposed attachments will sit within that pattern. A discreet result depends on an appropriate plan for the person wearing it, not only on a product advertised as invisible. Discuss both the desired added length and the amount of fullness. A smaller addition may change the finish without requiring the same plan as a major length transformation.'],
['Agree on matching and finishing','Ask how the added hair will be matched to the color and movement of your natural lengths. A dimensional color may need more than one shade within the plan. Clarify whether color work, a blending cut and styling are included in the estimate or separately booked. If you expect to change your natural color soon, mention it before hair is ordered. The consultation should describe the finished outline as well as the attachment method.'],
['Review washing and product placement','Ask for the precise care instructions for the tape system selected by the stylist. Product use near attachments, detangling and drying should follow that guidance rather than a generic extension routine. Explain your normal conditioning habits and whether you swim or exercise frequently. If you notice movement or discomfort, contact the salon before attempting an at-home adjustment. Follow the care schedule for the specific tape system used in your appointment.'],
['Budget for follow-up appointments','Confirm what happens at maintenance visits, how the hair is removed and repositioned when appropriate, and which costs apply. Ask whether the selected added hair can be reused and under what conditions. A lower initial installation price does not establish the total cost of ownership. Compare a tape-in plan with other methods using the same target length and fullness, then choose the plan that fits both your hair assessment and the appointments you can realistically maintain.']
],[['Are tape-ins automatically the right choice for fine hair?','No method is suitable for everyone. Ask the stylist to assess your density and the proposed placement.'],['Can I remove tape-ins myself?','Ask the salon about professional removal and the instructions for your specific method before attempting any adjustment.']]);

add('keratin-bond-extensions-pittsburgh','Keratin-Bond Extensions in Pittsburgh: Plan the Details','Extension methods','hair-extensions-pittsburgh',
'Keratin-bond extensions involve individual attachment sections rather than a continuous weft row. Craft Collective lists keratin bond among its extension methods. A consultation should clarify the exact system, the quantity and placement required, the maintenance expectations and the professional removal plan.',[
['Discuss individual placement','Ask how the small attachment sections would be distributed to create the result you want. Added length and added density require different planning questions. Show the stylist your usual parting and how you wear the hair off your face. The proposed placement should consider the natural hair available to support and cover the attachments. Avoid choosing the method solely because a photograph looks seamless on someone with a different density or haircut.'],
['Compare the goal with other methods','Use the same desired length and fullness when discussing bonds, tape-ins and a weft-based plan. Ask about the differences in movement, visibility in your hairstyles, care and appointment commitment. The method name alone cannot tell you which will feel most practical in everyday life. If your goal is primarily volume around a particular section, ask whether the chosen method addresses that area appropriately and whether a haircut adjustment would change the required amount of added hair.'],
['Understand installation and removal','Confirm the estimated installation time and whether preparation, color matching and a blending cut are part of the proposed service. Ask how and when the attachments are professionally removed or replaced under the selected system. Ask whether the selected hair can be reused and what return schedule applies. Those are product-specific and stylist-specific questions to resolve before paying for the initial service or ordering the hair.'],
['Make the care plan practical','Explain how often you wash, whether you swim and how you normally detangle or use heat tools. Ask for a demonstration of handling around individual attachments and a clear instruction for contacting the salon if a section slips or feels uncomfortable. Do not attempt to bond loose sections at home. Request the complete initial estimate and the likely follow-up costs. An informed decision includes both the desired appearance and the work required to maintain and remove it appropriately.']
],[['Is this the same as a keratin smoothing treatment?','No. Keratin-bond extensions are an attachment method for added hair; smoothing is a separate hair service.'],['Are all keratin-bond systems maintained the same way?','No universal schedule is established here. Confirm the exact product, care and removal instructions with the selected stylist.']]);

add('bridal-hair-trial-pittsburgh','Bridal Hair Trials in Pittsburgh: Test the Wedding-Day Plan','Bridal and events','bridal-hair-pittsburgh',
'A bridal hair trial should test more than whether a style looks good in a photograph. Craft Collective offers bridal consultations, trials and wedding-day styling. Use the trial to examine the shape, accessories, comfort and schedule of the hairstyle you intend to wear on the actual day.',[
['Bring references that explain your priorities','Choose a few photographs and identify the details you like: the height at the crown, softness around the face, a polished or textured finish, and whether the hair is fully up or partly down. Bring images of your dress neckline if useful to the discussion. Too many unrelated references can make the goal unclear. Tell the stylist which feature matters most so the trial has an agreed direction before styling begins.'],
['Include the veil and accessories','Bring the actual accessories when possible and ask how they will be positioned and secured. A veil, comb or hairpiece can change the placement of an updo. If someone else will remove the veil later, ask for a demonstration of that step. Discuss whether clip-in or other added hair is proposed and confirm the cost and preparation separately. Do not assume an accessory shown in an inspiration photograph can be placed identically in your chosen style.'],
['Evaluate the finish beyond the chair','Look at the shape from the front, sides and back. Take photographs with your permission and note how the style feels when you move and turn your head. Tell the stylist if something pulls or feels too tight rather than assuming discomfort is unavoidable. The trial provides a chance to adjust the design. A photograph records appearance, but it does not by itself establish how comfortable the style will be over an entire wedding day.'],
['Record the decisions for the wedding appointment','Confirm the final style, any changes after the trial, preparation instructions and the wedding-day appointment location. Ask how much time to allow and whether the trial and day-of styling are quoted separately. Craft Collective’s FAQ recommends planning bridal bookings ahead of the date; contact the salon about current availability. A completed trial does not automatically reserve the wedding appointment, travel arrangement or bridal-party capacity unless those details have been confirmed.']
],[['When should I book a trial?','Contact the salon early to plan around your wedding date and current availability. The selected stylist can advise when the trial fits the larger schedule.'],['Does a trial reserve wedding-day styling?','Confirm both appointments and any required arrangements directly. Do not assume one booking automatically includes the other.']]);

add('curly-haircuts-pittsburgh','Curly Haircuts in Pittsburgh: Shape, Texture and Booking','Specialist services','haircuts-pittsburgh',
'A useful curly haircut appointment starts with the way your curls behave on an ordinary day. Bring a photograph of your usual finish, not only a salon-styled reference. Craft Collective offers haircut consultations in North Hills and Canonsburg; call to match your texture and desired shape with the appropriate stylist.',[
['Choose the shape before the technique','Start by identifying where you want volume and where you want less weight. A rounded shape, a longer outline and a fringe create different decisions around the face and crown. Show the stylist which parts of a reference you like. Ask how that shape translates to your own curl pattern, density and current length. Hair that falls in several different patterns may need different treatment through the outline rather than a uniform amount removed everywhere.'],
['Discuss dry cutting and wet cutting','A dry cut can help a stylist observe the way curls sit in their current finish. Wet cutting can provide a controlled starting point for other shaping approaches. Some appointments combine both. The useful question is which approach suits your goal and how your hair should be prepared. Do not assume that every stylist offers a particular branded curl-cut method. Confirm the technique and preparation directly before booking, especially if you normally straighten your hair.'],
['Explain shrinkage and styling habits','Tell the stylist whether you wear your hair natural, stretched, diffused or straightened. Describe how the length changes after drying and which areas tend to lose definition. Ask how the proposed layers will look in the finishes you actually wear. If you alternate between curls and a smooth blowout, discuss both before cutting. Bring the products you use by name and describe the order of application so the conversation can distinguish shape from styling.'],
['Leave with a repeatable finish','Ask the stylist to show the part of the routine that makes the largest difference to the new shape. That might involve sectioning, product distribution or handling during drying. You do not need to purchase an entire new routine to have a useful conversation. Before leaving, agree on the signs that the outline needs refreshing, what appointment to book next and whether the maintenance visit differs from your first curly consultation.']
],[['Should I arrive with freshly washed curls?','Ask the selected stylist. Preparation depends on the cutting approach; arrive in the condition they request rather than assuming every curly appointment follows the same rules.'],['Can I book a certified curl-cut method?','Call to confirm the particular method and stylist. This page does not claim a DevaCut, Rezo or other certification for the team.']]);

add('gray-blending-pittsburgh','Gray Blending in Pittsburgh: Coverage or a Softer Grow-Out?','Specialist services','hair-color-pittsburgh',
'Gray blending aims to soften the contrast between gray strands and the rest of your color. It is a different goal from covering every gray hair. At a Craft Collective color consultation, explain whether you want to conceal gray, introduce it gradually or make your existing silver more prominent.',[
['Decide how much gray you want to see','Use your current parting and hairline as the starting point. Some guests want a softer border when roots grow; others want a noticeable transition toward their natural silver. Those goals can require different combinations of highlights, lowlights, tone adjustments or coverage. Bring photographs that show the root area as well as the overall finish. A picture of silver lengths does not explain the work required to move from a dark permanent-color base.'],
['Account for your existing color','Tell the colorist what has been applied at home and in salons, including the dates and whether the color was repeatedly pulled through the lengths. Previously colored sections may behave differently from new growth. The first consultation should establish what can change now and what needs a gradual approach. Do not promise yourself an all-silver result in one visit based on another person’s transformation. Your history and condition determine the realistic first step.'],
['Compare blending with complete coverage','Coverage may suit someone who wants a consistent base shade and accepts regular root attention. Blending may suit someone comfortable seeing gray within a dimensional finish. Neither choice is automatically maintenance-free. Tonal refreshes, adjustments to brightness and haircut maintenance can still matter. Ask the stylist to explain how each option looks as new growth appears, then choose the version you would enjoy between visits as well as on appointment day.'],
['Make the return plan part of the decision','Discuss how often you want to come back and whether your priority is the hairline, the parting or the full head. Ask what the first estimate includes and what later visits may involve. If the goal is to stop coloring eventually, request a transition plan rather than an indefinite sequence of corrections. Photographs of your progress can help you and the stylist reassess the blend without assuming each return visit needs more lightening.']
],[['Is gray blending the same as gray coverage?','No. Coverage aims to conceal gray within the chosen shade; blending aims to reduce contrast while allowing some gray to remain visible.'],['Can dark colored hair become silver in one appointment?','That cannot be determined from a trend photo. An individual consultation should assess the existing color and condition before establishing a plan.']]);

add('color-correction-pittsburgh','Color Correction in Pittsburgh: Plan the First Appointment','Specialist services','hair-color-pittsburgh',
'Uneven tone, unexpected darkness and patchy brightness are different color problems. Craft Collective lists corrective color among its services and requires an in-person consultation for these appointments. Describe the result you have now and the result you want before selecting a standard color slot.',[
['Identify the issue precisely','Point out the areas that concern you: the roots, a darker band, warm mids, lighter ends or an uneven overall tone. A clear current photograph can help begin the discussion, but an in-person assessment gives the colorist more information. Explain whether you dislike the depth, the warmth, the placement or the contrast. Saying only that the color is wrong can lead to a different proposed solution from the one you actually want.'],
['Bring the full color history','Include salon color, box color, direct dyes, lightening, straightening and other chemical services you remember. If you do not know the formula, say so. Do not treat all at-home dyes as chemically identical or assume that every unwanted shade can be removed in the same way. The colorist may recommend assessing a small section before a larger change. Ask what information they need before the consultation and whether product packaging would be helpful.'],
['Agree on an achievable first result','A correction may mean balancing tone, reducing contrast or changing only part of the hair before pursuing a lighter result. Ask what the stylist recommends preserving and what they recommend postponing. If you have an event deadline, share it early. A modest adjustment that suits your current condition can be more practical than trying to reproduce a major transformation within a single available time block. The first appointment should have a specific agreed goal.'],
['Confirm the quote and next steps','Correction pricing depends on the work required; this page does not establish an hourly rate or fixed package. Ask how the estimate is calculated, which finishing services it includes and how a change of plan would be discussed. Clarify whether the proposed result requires later appointments and how those differ from maintenance. After the service, follow the stylist’s product and handling advice for the actual work performed rather than adding another home correction immediately.']
],[['Should I book normal highlights to fix uneven color?','Call first. Corrective work may require a consultation and a different appointment block from routine highlights.'],['Can I get a guaranteed correction price online?','An individual assessment is needed. Ask for the estimate, its inclusions and any circumstances that could change the plan before agreeing to the service.']]);

add('lived-in-color-pittsburgh','Lived-In Color in Pittsburgh: A Plan for Softer Regrowth','Specialist services','hair-color-pittsburgh',
'Lived-in color describes a blended appearance designed to remain wearable as roots grow. Craft Collective’s published menu includes lived-in color, highlights, lowlights, smudging and toning. The right combination depends on your starting color and how much brightness you want near your face and through the lengths.',[
['Define your version of low maintenance','Some guests mean fewer major lightening visits; others mean less contrast around the hairline or a finish that looks natural when air-dried. Explain which issue matters most. A rooted blonde can still need tone attention, and a subtle brunette can still need coverage if gray concealment is part of the goal. Ask the colorist which elements are doing the work in the design so you understand what may need refreshing later.'],
['Choose brightness and placement deliberately','Show the stylist your ordinary parting, the way you tie your hair back and the areas you want to brighten. Face-framing pieces, interior dimension and brighter ends contribute differently to the final impression. A dramatic money piece may create a different upkeep commitment from a soft overall blend. Ask to see the intended balance before agreeing to additional brightness simply because it appears in the inspiration image.'],
['Separate the root blend from the technique','Balayage, foils, lowlights and root adjustments can all contribute to a lived-in look. The appearance does not require one universal technique. Discuss the desired depth at the root, the transition through the mids and the tone of the ends. If you already have a very light base, adding dimension may be part of the plan. If your hair is dark or previously colored, creating brightness may require a different sequence.'],
['Plan distinct maintenance visits','Ask which visits maintain tone, which address growth and which make a larger change to placement. Repeating the first full appointment is not always the intended maintenance plan. Explain your budget for the year and how often you can visit a Pittsburgh studio. Agree on what you should notice as the color grows and what would be a reason to contact the salon sooner. That creates a practical definition of low maintenance for your hair.']
],[['Is lived-in color always balayage?','No. It is a description of the finish; the stylist may use several techniques to create the blend.'],['Does it eliminate every maintenance appointment?','No. Ask about tone, root contrast and the return plan for your chosen result.']]);

add('hair-gloss-toner-pittsburgh','Hair Gloss and Toner in Pittsburgh: Choose the Right Refresh','Specialist services','hair-color-pittsburgh',
'A gloss or toner appointment can refine the appearance of existing color without repeating every step of a major color service. Craft Collective lists glossing and toning on its menu. Explain whether you want a tonal adjustment, renewed shine or a change in overall depth so the stylist can recommend the appropriate appointment.',[
['Name the change you want to see','Warmth, dullness and a desire for a darker shade are different requests. Bring a current photograph in natural light and explain the area that bothers you. If you like the existing brightness but dislike its tone, say that explicitly. A color refresh should be based on the result you want rather than on a product name heard online. The stylist can explain whether a gloss or toner fits that request and what it can realistically change.'],
['Understand what a refresh cannot replace','A toner does not substitute for all the work required to create a much lighter starting point. If you want a major change in brightness, discuss lightening separately. Likewise, a shine-focused refresh is different from a plan to conceal new gray growth. Clarify whether the appointment maintains an existing result or changes the overall color direction. This helps avoid booking a short refresh when the intended work belongs in a longer color consultation.'],
['Consider differences through the lengths','Your roots, mids and ends may not begin at the same color or condition. Tell the stylist about previous lightening and the areas that seem to change tone sooner. A single reference image may not show those differences. Ask whether the proposed application addresses the full head or selected areas and whether the finish includes a blow-dry. Comparing photographs before and after in similar lighting makes the visible adjustment easier to understand.'],
['Book the refresh within a larger color plan','If you already see a Craft Collective colorist, explain when the last service took place and what has changed since. Ask which menu option fits the refresh and what home routine is appropriate for the actual formula used. Avoid relying on repeated at-home toning to solve a color concern without showing the stylist the result. Before leaving, clarify when to review the tone again and whether the next appointment should include new lightening or only another refresh.']
],[['Are gloss and toner interchangeable labels?','Salons may use the terms differently. Describe the desired change and ask which formula and appointment suit it.'],['Will a toner make dark hair much lighter?','A tonal refresh is different from a major lightening service. Discuss the starting shade and desired brightness with the colorist.']]);

add('kids-haircuts-pittsburgh','Kids Haircuts in Pittsburgh: Prepare a Comfortable Visit','Specialist services','haircuts-pittsburgh',
'Craft Collective’s haircut menu includes kids cuts. A useful booking conversation explains the child’s age, current hair, desired shape and any needs that could affect the appointment. North Hills booking is available online; call if you need help selecting the right service or planning a Canonsburg visit.',[
['Describe the appointment rather than only the age','Tell the salon whether the goal is a first trim, a regular maintenance cut or a significant change in length. Longer hair, a new fringe and a shorter shape can involve different conversations. Show a simple reference and explain what the child wants to keep. If the child has a strong preference about length, discuss it together before arriving so the stylist is not asked to resolve conflicting instructions while the appointment is underway.'],
['Share comfort needs before booking','Explain whether the child is uneasy about clippers, loud dryers, washing or remaining in the chair. Ask which parts of the service can be discussed in advance and what the salon recommends. Do not assume a particular sensory accommodation is available without checking. If a parent needs to remain nearby, confirm that arrangement. A clear conversation gives the team an opportunity to advise on the most practical appointment rather than treating every kids cut identically.'],
['Keep the desired style manageable','Think about school mornings, sports, tangles and how much styling the child will tolerate. A picture-perfect finish may require steps that do not fit the household routine. Ask how the shape will fall without daily heat styling and how the fringe or face-framing pieces will behave. If the child usually wears a ponytail, mention that before substantial length is removed. Practical details help turn an inspiration image into a style the child will actually enjoy.'],
['Confirm inclusions and the next visit','Ask whether the selected kids appointment includes washing, drying and styling, and confirm the price with the salon. Confirm the applicable age category and current rate before reserving the service. After the cut, ask for a simple detangling or styling demonstration if needed and agree on what to book when the shape needs refreshing. If the first visit is challenging, discuss what could make the next appointment easier rather than assuming every future visit must follow the same pattern.']
],[['Is there a fixed age cutoff for a kids haircut?','Confirm the applicable booking category with the salon. No age cutoff is established by this guide.'],['Can I request a quieter appointment?','Call to discuss the child’s needs and current scheduling options. A particular accommodation or quiet time is not guaranteed.']]);

add('balayage-prices-pittsburgh','Balayage Prices in Pittsburgh: Compare the Complete Quote','Pricing and value','balayage-pittsburgh',
'A balayage price is useful only when you know what the appointment includes. Craft Collective quotes balayage according to the individual hair and desired change. Before comparing Pittsburgh salons, establish whether each quote describes a similar amount of lightening, toning and finishing.',[
['Separate first-time color from maintenance','Creating a new placement on previously colored hair can be a different appointment from refreshing an existing blend. Tell the salon when your last color service took place, what was applied and whether you want more brightness or the same result maintained. A price listed for a partial service should not be compared directly with a full transformation. Ask which areas are included and what the stylist expects to achieve in that visit.'],
['List every component of the finish','Ask whether the proposed total includes the lightening service, toner or gloss, root adjustment, haircut and blow-dry when relevant. Not every plan needs all of those components, and salon menus organize them differently. The goal is to understand your proposed service rather than assume that every add-on is necessary. If a reference shows a new haircut as well as new color, identify the two changes separately in the estimate.'],
['Budget for the result between visits','The cost of the first appointment is only part of the decision. Ask whether the upkeep plan involves a tone refresh, a partial lightening visit or another full service. Explain how often you want to return and which visible changes you would be comfortable with. A softer root transition can change the maintenance approach, but it does not promise a fixed number of appointments for every guest. Ask for a schedule suited to your own goal.'],
['Request the Craft Collective estimate','Share current and desired photographs and specify North Hills or Canonsburg. The salon can advise whether to begin with a consultation or a particular balayage booking. Confirm the selected stylist, included finish and total before agreeing to the service. Confirm the current Craft Collective price directly with the salon. An individual quote is the reliable next step, particularly when previous color or a large brightness change is involved.']
],[['Does this page list Craft Collective’s balayage price?','No fixed price is established here. The salon’s published FAQ recommends an individual consultation and quote.'],['What should I compare between two balayage quotes?','Compare the starting hair, coverage area, intended brightness, toner, cut, finish and recommended maintenance.']]);

add('highlights-prices-pittsburgh','Highlights Prices in Pittsburgh: Partial, Full and Face-Framing','Pricing and value','highlights-pittsburgh',
'Partial highlights, full highlights and face-framing brightness describe different scopes of work. A useful Pittsburgh highlights quote identifies the coverage area and the finished result. Craft Collective offers customized highlights and lowlights; confirm the plan before choosing a menu item solely by its opening price.',[
['Ask what partial and full mean on the menu','A partial service may focus on selected areas, while a full service covers more of the head. The precise sectioning varies by salon and stylist. Ask which areas will be lightened, particularly if you often wear your hair up or change your parting. Someone seeking brightness underneath the hair may need a different plan from someone focused only on the face. The service label alone does not establish the finished appearance.'],
['Consider highlights together with lowlights','If the hair is already very bright, the desired dimension may involve adding depth rather than placing more light pieces everywhere. Describe whether your current result feels too flat, too warm or too dark. A stylist can explain how highlights and lowlights contribute separately. Do not assume that a larger number of foils is the best solution for every color goal. The estimate should reflect the actual work proposed, including any tonal finishing.'],
['Check the estimate against your history','Fresh regrowth, old highlights and repeatedly colored lengths can call for different placement decisions. Tell the stylist about recent salon and at-home color and ask whether the appointment is routine maintenance or a larger adjustment. A face-framing quote is not an equivalent substitute for a full-head plan. If budget is a concern, ask which smaller first step would still achieve a result you would enjoy rather than reducing the appointment without discussing the effect.'],
['Understand the refresh after the first visit','Ask whether future visits would repeat the same service or alternate with a smaller refresh. Confirm whether toning and a blow-dry are included in each estimate, and whether cutting is booked separately. At Craft Collective, request the quote for the stylist and location you intend to use. Use the individual quote and its inclusions when comparing the proposed work with another salon.']
],[['Is a partial highlight always enough?','It depends on the coverage you want and the way you wear your hair. Ask the stylist to identify the areas included.'],['Should I choose the cheapest highlights slot?','Choose an appointment that fits the proposed work, then confirm its complete estimate and finishing services.']]);

add('haircut-prices-pittsburgh','Haircut Prices in Pittsburgh: What to Check Before Booking','Pricing and value','haircuts-pittsburgh',
'A haircut quote should identify the appointment you will receive, including the consultation and finish. Craft Collective offers women’s, men’s and kids cuts. Discuss the shape you want and any change in length before comparing the total with another Pittsburgh salon.',[
['Distinguish a trim from a new shape','A maintenance trim preserves an existing outline, while a substantial change may require a longer consultation and different shaping decisions. Explain whether you want to keep the length, add layers, create a fringe or move to a shorter cut. Do not call a major change a trim simply to fit a smaller booking category. A clear description helps the salon select the right appointment and provide a useful estimate for the intended result.'],
['Confirm the shampoo and finish','Ask what is included in the selected haircut appointment: washing, conditioning, drying, styling and any additional finishing. A dry cut and a cut with a blowout are different comparisons even if both menus say haircut. If you want to see the shape worn naturally and styled smooth, discuss that before booking. The salon can clarify whether both finishes fit the proposed time block and whether an additional service applies.'],
['Match the stylist to your goal','A lower or higher listed price does not tell you who is most suitable for a particular shape. Review the team and examples of work, then ask who regularly handles the kind of change you want. For curls, fine hair, a short crop or a first kids visit, include those details in the booking conversation. Confirm the chosen stylist’s current price directly; ask how the selected appointment and stylist affect the estimate.'],
['Plan the upkeep you will actually use','Ask how the outline will change as it grows and what a future maintenance visit would involve. A precise short shape and long layers can have different refresh needs. Explain how much styling you want to do at home and whether the hair must tie back for work or exercise. The useful value comparison is a cut you can wear confidently between appointments, with a booking category and maintenance plan that fit your routine.']
],[['Are all Craft Collective haircut prices identical?','Confirm the quote for the stylist and appointment you select. Ask for the price of the specific appointment you intend to book.'],['Can I request a price before the haircut begins?','Ask for the appointment estimate and clarify its inclusions before agreeing to the service.']]);
